  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n\n- $y=2$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is also a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line.",
